---
import Content from '../layouts/Content.astro';
import { company, site, power, network, compute } from '../data/site';

/* Derived from the record rather than retyped. The monthly figure is an
   unverified commercial input, not a confirmed quote, and is labelled as one
   wherever it appears. */
const netMonthly = Number(network.cost.replace(/[^\d.]/g, ''));
const netGbps = Number((network.service.match(/[\d.]+/) ?? ['0'])[0]);
const netAnnual = netMonthly * 12;
const netPerGbps = netMonthly / netGbps;
/* "100 Gbps" out of "100 Gbps asymmetrical", so the headline figure and its
   qualifier can be set at different sizes without retyping either. */
const netHeadline = network.service.split(' ').slice(0, 2).join(' ');
---

<Content
  title={`Colocation and GPU capacity at ${site.designation} | ${company.full}`}
  description={`Colocation and dedicated compute in ${site.name}. Buildings A, B and C total ${site.buildingArea.toLocaleString()} sq ft of existing building area behind a shared ${power.service} service. First compute is a ${site.powerOn}.`}
  kicker="Colocation and dedicated compute"
  heading="Two ways to buy, both reviewed before they are offered."
  lede={`Bring hardware you own, or take a dedicated allocation on the planned nodes. Either way what you get first is an engineering review: ${site.buildingArea.toLocaleString()} sq ft of existing building area is a starting point, not a commissioned data hall.`}
  meta={[
    { label: 'Existing building area', value: `${site.buildingArea.toLocaleString()} sq ft`, note: 'Buildings A, B and C, gross' },
    { label: 'Shared service', value: power.service, note: 'From Building C', tone: 'gold' },
    { label: 'Network', value: netHeadline, note: `${network.provider}, planned` },
    { label: 'First compute', value: site.powerOn, note: 'Target, not a confirmed date', tone: 'signal' },
  ]}
>
  <section aria-labelledby="two-ways" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">01</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Two ways to buy</span></p>
    <h2 id="two-ways">Space and power, or capacity on our machines</h2>

    <div class="cards">
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__tag mono-label">Option A</p>
        <h3>Colocation</h3>
        <p>
          You bring the hardware. We provide space in Buildings A, B or C, power from the
          shared {power.service} service, cooling, network and hands on site. You keep
          ownership of your accelerators and your software stack. Your equipment has to
          fit documented rack dimensions and loading, feeder limits, cooling and access
          constraints — which is what the review establishes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sensible for anyone who has already bought GPUs, or who has a procurement
          relationship they do not want to give up.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="card">
        <p class="card__tag mono-label">Option B</p>
        <h3>GPU capacity</h3>
        <p>
          You take a dedicated allocation on the planned fleet — {compute.systems},
          {' '}{compute.gpus} GPUs in total. {compute.status}. No capital outlay and no
          hardware lead time of your own. Full specification is on the
          {' '}<a href="/compute">compute page</a>.
        </p>
        <p>
          Suited to inference, computer vision, rendering and workload-specific
          fine-tuning. Training and larger models need a benchmark first.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="power-terms" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">02</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Power</span></p>
    <h2 id="power-terms">Power is the product; everything else is logistics</h2>

    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>
          Colocation is sold by the kilowatt, not by the square foot, because power is
          what runs out first. What is standing at {site.designation} is a shared
          {' '}{power.service} distribution originating at Building C and serving A, B and
          C — one rating across the three, not three separate supplies.
        </p>
        <p>
          What that converts to in usable IT kilowatts is not something arithmetic can
          settle. Continuous-load limits, utility allocation, feeder ratings, cooling,
          existing loads and redundancy all sit between a service rating and an
          allocation. {power.allocation}. Ask us for one and we will run it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Energy is {power.rate}. How that reaches a tenant invoice —
          metered pass-through, an all-in rate per kW, or a blend — is
          {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: tenant power billing model and whether energy is passed through at cost]</span>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="figures">
        <div>
          <dt>Shared service</dt>
          <dd class="is-gold">{power.service}<span class="sub">{power.serviceScope}</span></dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Tenant allocation</dt>
          <dd>On review<span class="sub">{power.allocation}. Request an engineering allocation and we will size it against the actual service.</span></dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="density" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">03</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Density and cooling</span></p>
    <h2 id="density">Density, and what it asks of the cooling</h2>

    <p>
      An air-cooled hall stops being able to remove heat long before a modern accelerator
      rack stops wanting to produce it. That is the physical reason legacy colocation
      cannot simply be repurposed for AI training: the electrical feed may exist, but the
      thermal path does not.
    </p>
    <p>
      The planned first phase is {compute.systems}, at {compute.load}. Existing HVAC
      serves the data-center buildings; building-specific capacity and operating limits
      are being documented. The cooling method has not been
      fixed yet, and we are not going to claim one before it is:
      {' '}<span class="todo">{compute.cooling}</span>. What the building will support at
      higher density is a separate question, answered below.
    </p>

    <dl class="spec">
      <div><dt>Cooling method</dt><dd>{compute.cooling}</dd></div>
      <div><dt>Design density</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: supported kW per rack, and the rack count at that density]</span><span class="note">We will publish a measured figure once the loop is commissioned, not a design maximum from a datasheet.</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Heat rejection</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: heat rejection method — dry coolers, adiabatic, or chilled water — and whether it consumes water]</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Coolant loop</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: CDU vendor and model, loop redundancy, supply temperature]</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Air-cooled space</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: whether any air-cooled racks are offered for storage, networking and head nodes]</span></dd></div>
      <div><dt>Efficiency</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: measured PUE once Phase 1A is live]</span></dd></div>
    </dl>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="network" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">04</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Network</span></p>
    <h2 id="network">{network.service} into a field in Oklahoma</h2>

    <div class="split">
      <div>
        <p>
          Rural sites are usually dismissed on connectivity before anyone looks at the
          power. This one carries a signed quote from {network.provider} for
          {' '}{network.service} — planned, and asymmetrical rather than a shared
          business broadband product. {network.status}.
        </p>
        <p>
          At {network.cost}, that is roughly ${netPerGbps.toFixed(2)} per Gbps per month
          committed, or ${netAnnual.toLocaleString()} a year, with installation waived.
          The figure is here because a buyer will work it out anyway.
        </p>
        <p>
          What that circuit does not yet include is a second one. Carrier diversity,
          transit and peering arrangements, and whether cross-connects are available to
          other tenants are all open:
          {' '}<span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: second carrier / diverse path, IP transit arrangements, cross-connect availability and pricing]</span>.
        </p>
      </div>

      <dl class="spec">
        <div><dt>Provider</dt><dd>{network.provider}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Service</dt><dd>{network.service}<span class="note">{network.status}</span></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Cost</dt><dd>{network.cost}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Term</dt><dd>{network.term}</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Diversity</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: is there a second physical path]</span></dd></div>
        <div><dt>Tenant allocation</dt><dd><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: how bandwidth is allocated or resold to tenants]</span></dd></div>
      </dl>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="terms" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">05</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Commercial terms</span></p>
    <h2 id="terms">Terms, including the ones we have not set</h2>

    <p>
      This table is deliberately half empty. The filled rows are contractual facts about
      the site. The empty ones are decisions that have not been made, and we would rather
      you negotiated them with us than read an invented number here.
    </p>

    <div class="scroller" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Colocation terms, scrollable">
      <table>
        <caption>Colocation terms, {site.designation}</caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Term</th>
            <th scope="col">Position</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Location</th><td>{site.address} — {site.county}, Oklahoma</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Available space</th><td>Building 1, {site.buildings[0].sqft.toLocaleString()} sq ft. Buildings 2 and 3 available as expansion shells.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Service availability</th><td>{site.powerOn} power-on target</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Energy</th><td>{power.rate} at the site</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Cooling</th><td>{compute.cooling}</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Network</th><td>{network.service}, {network.provider} — planned</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Minimum commitment</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: minimum kW or rack count per contract]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Contract length</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: standard and minimum term lengths]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Pricing</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: $ per kW per month, and whether space is billed separately]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Escalator</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: annual escalator, if any]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Deposit</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: security deposit or letter of credit requirement]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Uptime SLA</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: uptime commitment and service credits]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Remote hands</th><td>Offered. <span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: included hours per month, hourly rate beyond that, response time, and 24×7 vs business-hours coverage]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Site access</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: escorted vs unescorted access, hours, and the access request process]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Physical security</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: perimeter, access control, CCTV retention, and whether the site is manned]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Compliance</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: audits or attestations planned, if any. None are claimed today.]</span></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Shipping and receiving</th><td><span class="todo">[PLACEHOLDER: receiving hours, dock or lift-gate access, and staging space for tenant deliveries]</span></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout" style="margin-top: var(--s-6);">
      <p>
        <strong>No certifications are claimed on this page.</strong> {company.name} holds
        no audit attestation it has not named. If a compliance requirement is a gate for
        you, say so in the first conversation and we will tell you honestly whether the
        {' '}{site.powerOn} timeline can carry it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="hall" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">06</span><span class="eyebrow__t">The hall</span></p>
    <h2 id="hall">Building 1, before fit-out</h2>

    <figure class="photo">
      <div class="photo__frame">
        <svg class="photo__icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="28" height="28" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" aria-hidden="true">
          <rect x="2.5" y="6" width="19" height="14" rx="2" />
          <circle cx="12" cy="13" r="3.5" />
          <path d="M8.5 6l1.4-2.2h4.2L15.5 6" />
          <path d="M3 3l18 18" />
        </svg>
        <p class="photo__state">Photograph not taken yet</p>
      </div>
      <figcaption>
        <b>[PHOTO NEEDED]</b> Interior of Building 1 as it stands, plus the electrical
        room. There is no photograph of a populated hall because the hall is not populated
        yet — power-on is {site.powerOn}. We will not borrow someone else's data center
        interior to fill this frame.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="is-end" aria-labelledby="reserve" data-reveal>
    <p class="eyebrow" aria-hidden="true"><span class="eyebrow__n">07</span><span class="eyebrow__t">Next</span></p>
    <h2 id="reserve">Reserve before Phase 1A fills</h2>
    <p>
      The planned first phase is {compute.gpus} GPUs across {compute.servers} servers. Tell us the load in
      kilowatts, the shape of the workload, and the date you need it live, and we will
      come back with a term sheet against real capacity rather than a waiting list.
    </p>
    <div class="actions">
      <a class="btn" href="/contact">Reserve capacity</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/power">Why there is no queue</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Content>
